Looking at your code, here are the key issues I notice:
Critical Problem:
Your car method should be __init__ - this is Python's constructor method. Right now, your class won't initialize properly when you create a new Car object. Without __init__, you'd need to manually call the car method after creating an instance, which isn't standard practice.
Logic Issues:

Your hired method checks the status but doesn't actually change it - it only prints whether the car is available or not. You probably want this method to set self.hire = True.
Your returned method has confusing logic. When self.hire is True, it says "Car is hired" (which seems like just a status check), and when False it says "Car is returned". This method should probably set self.hire = False to mark the car as returned.
The show_detail method doesn't display the year and model that you're storing - it only shows the hire status.

Design Consideration:
The naming is a bit inconsistent - you have a boolean called hire but methods called hired and returned. It might be clearer to have methods like hire_car() and return_car() that actually change the state, plus maybe a check_availability() method that just displays status.
The overall structure is on the right track, but the methods need to actually modify the hire attribute rather than just checking and printing its current value.